{# game description #}# Meeting Participation Decision-Making: Maximizing Your Utility
Welcome to the “World of Six” experiment! Your task is to maximize your positive utility in a meeting. You need to predict the number of participants in the meeting and use this to decide whether to attend or not.

## How utility is calculated
Your utility will be calculated according to the following formula:
utility = standalone_value + network_effect * participant_number - price

- utility: This is what you need to maximize. If you choose not to participate, your utility will be 0.
- standalone_value: This is the intrinsic value of your participation in the meeting, your standalone value is different from the other participants, but everyone knows the distribution.
- network_effect: The more participants you have, the more you get. This value is the same for all participants.
- participant_number: The number of people who ultimately choose to participate in the session. For each round, this number is determined only after everyone has made their expectations.
- price (base cost): The cost of participating in the session. This is the same for all participants in each round.

## Experimental Procedure
1. **INDEPENDENT ESTIMATE**: You will need to independently estimate the number of participants for this session. At this point, you will not know the choices of others.
2. **Result Announcement**: After all participants have made their choices, the system will announce the actual number of participants.
3. **Utility Settlement**: According to the utility calculation formula, you will get your final benefit. If you choose not to participate, your payoff will be zero.

